.TH symedit "" "Command"
.SH NAME
symedit \- GUI symbol editor for the vellum library
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsymedit [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIlibrary\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
symedit -- GUI symbol editor for the vellum library
Usage:	symedit [ options ] [ library-file ]
Keys:	s/c/a/p/t/v/e arm Seg/Circ/Arc/Pin/Char/Move/Del,
	r/m rotate/mirror the whole symbol, f copy-from,
	i import a drawing, k check the library,
	x delete selection, u undo, Esc back to Seg
.HE
.B symedit
edits a
.B vellum
symbol library file: draw a part once here \- a lab instrument, a
relay, a connector \- and place it in every schematic afterwards.
The optional
.I library
argument names the file to edit (the Edit button in
.B vellum
passes the current library); the default is the user scratch
library, /usr/vellum/etc/symbols.
The status line shows the file's name in brackets.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop; see
.BR zview .
.PP
The canvas shows one symbol magnified, dotted at every quarter grid
unit with heavier marks on whole grid units.
The cross is the ORIGIN: the point
.B vellum
places, rotates and mirrors the symbol around.
Under the tool cells sits a true-scale preview \- exactly what the
palette and canvas of
.B vellum
will draw.
.PP
The tools:
.DS
.ta 8n
	\fBSeg\fR	drag draws a segment, snapped to quarter units
	\fBCirc\fR	drag from the centre; radius in quarter units
	\fBArc\fR	drag from the centre to the START point (radius
		and start angle), then drag once more to sweep
		out the end; angles snap to 5 degrees
	\fBPin\fR	click places a connection pin, snapped to WHOLE
		grid units \- pins are where wires snap in \fBvellum\fR.
		A second click on a pin asks for its NAME, which
		the netlist export prints (Q1.B instead of Q1.2),
		and its TYPE \- in, out, power or bidirectional \-
		which the B-checkR electrical rules test (two
		outputs on a net conflict, an output must not
		drive a power rail, all-input nets are undriven)
	\fBChar\fR	click asks for one small-font character
	\fBMove\fR	drag an element to move it; a drag on empty
		canvas marquee-selects, and the set then moves
		as one and deletes with \fBx\fR
	\fBDel\fR	click removes the nearest element
.DE
The
.B r
and
.B m
keys rotate (a quarter turn) and mirror the WHOLE symbol around the
origin \- draw one gate half, mirror it.
The
.B f
key copies a symbol IN from another library file as the starting
point (asks for the file and the code; the buffer is replaced, the
code and prefix stay).
The
.B i
key IMPORTS A DRAWING as the stencil (VELLUM.md sec. 55): it asks for
the
.B .d
file, the grid point that becomes the symbol origin (blank = the
drawing's first
.B N
marker) and the scale, then converts under exactly the rules
.B "vellum -mksym"
uses \- lines, wires, boxes and polylines become segments, circles and
arcs themselves, a ONE-CHARACTER text a glyph, and the
.B N
markers become the pins in file order (an
.B N
called
.B \-
is an unnamed pin).
Shapes, connectors, dimensions, nested symbols and longer text have no
stencil form and are dropped.
Drawing at
.B "-scale 4"
makes one drawing unit a quarter unit, which is the detail a hand-cut
stencil has.
The buffer is replaced and
.B u
undoes it, so the promote-then-touch-up workflow is one program.
.PP
The
.B k
key CHECKS the open library: the same findings
.B "vellum -symcheck"
reports \- a code defined twice, a symbol with no geometry, a
designator prefix with no pins, a pin off a whole unit, a pin name that
repeats, a pin left untyped in a library that types the others \- a
page at a time, More wrapping round.
.PP

The
.B u
key UNDOES the last edit (one level; pressing it again redoes) and
.B Esc
cancels in two stages: the first returns to Seg keeping the
selection, a second drops the selection too.
The two triangle cells under the preview step to the previous and
next symbol in the library, keeping any edits in memory.
.PP
The window menu carries New (asks for the symbol's code and its
designator prefix), Open (by code; the dialog lists what the library
holds), Save and Help \- which opens this page in
.BR zman .
Switching symbols keeps every edit in memory;
.B Save
writes the whole library back to the file.
A running
.B vellum
reads the library only at start-up, so open a fresh one to see saved
changes.
.SH FILES
.DS
	/usr/vellum/etc/symbols	the user scratch library (default)
	/usr/vellum/sym/*.sym	the stock libraries (discrete, logic,
		flow, net, arch, pid, power, plan)
	/usr/vellum/etc/libs	which libraries vellum loads at start-up
.DE
The format is the plain text described in
.BR vellum ;
it can also be edited by hand.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B zview ,
.B zman
.SH NOTES
The library holds up to 12 symbols of up to 8 pins each.
Keep bodies within about \-20..40 quarter units horizontally and
\-20..20 vertically so they fit the palette cells sensibly.
